 ### Relightable 3D Gaussians
-#### definition
-A differentiable point-based rendering framework that enhances vanilla 3D Gaussians by associating extra properties (normal vectors, BRDF parameters, and incident lighting) to achieve photo-realistic relighting and BRDF decomposition from multi-view images [S1].
+**Definition**
+Relightable 3D Gaussians is a differentiable point-based rendering framework that enhances vanilla 3D Gaussians by associating extra properties (normal vectors, BRDF parameters, and incident lighting) to achieve photo-realistic relighting and BRDF decomposition from multi-view images [S1].
 
-#### step
-- Assign a normal vector $\mathbf{n}$, BRDF values, and incident light information to each 3D Gaussian point to model per-point light reflectance [S1].
-- Apply physically based rendering (PBR) to get a PBR color for each 3D Gaussian point, which is then alpha-composited to obtain the rendered color for the corresponding image pixel [S1].
-- Split the incident light into a global environment map and an indirect incident light field for robust material and lighting estimation [S1].
-- Introduce proper regularizations to enhance geometry and mitigate material-lighting ambiguity, including constraints on depth distribution, smoothness priors, and a lighting regularization [S1].
+**Step**
+1.  Assign a normal vector $\mathbf{n}$, BRDF values, and incident light information to each 3D Gaussian point to model per-point light reflectance [S1].
+2.  Apply physically based rendering (PBR) to get a PBR color for each 3D Gaussian point, which is then alpha-composited to obtain the rendered color for the corresponding image pixel [S1].
+3.  Split the incident light into a global environment map and an indirect incident light field for robust material and lighting estimation [S1].
+4.  Introduce proper regularizations to enhance geometry and mitigate material-lighting ambiguity, including constraints on depth distribution, smoothness priors, and a lighting regularization [S1].
+5.  Propose a novel ray tracing method based on the bounding volume hierarchy (BVH) for efficient visibility pre-computing, enabling realistic shadow effects and handling complex occlusion relationships [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The base 3D Gaussian Splatting representation [G] uses Spherical Harmonics for view-dependent color, which is efficient but limited in modeling complex materials and lighting. Relightable 3D Gaussians was introduced by Jian Gao et al. in 2024 to enable photo-realistic relighting and BRDF decomposition [S1]. This work enhances vanilla 3D Gaussians by associating extra properties, including normal vectors, BRDF parameters, and incident lighting from various directions. The framework achieves realistic relighting through physically based differentiable rendering and introduces a novel point-based ray tracing method with bounding volume hierarchies for efficient visibility pre-computation [S1].
+The base 3D Gaussian Splatting representation was introduced by Kerbl et al. [26] (referenced in [S1]), providing efficient differentiable rendering. Relightable 3D Gaussians was proposed by Gao et al. in 2024 to address the lack of physical material and lighting decomposition in the original framework [S1].
 
